  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nThe cold front has cleared the forecast area east this evening\nleaving temperatures in the 40s and light drizzle in its wake.\nContinued cold advection in NW flow is expected overnight as\nmore organized light rain and drizzle transitions to more NW\nflow induced light showers closer to the lakes. \n \nAloft, a closed low will meander around the Great Lakes through\nSaturday as cold advection in the low levels continues. \nEnsemble mean 850 mb temps dip to -1 to -3C as lapse rates \nsteepen and lake- effect showers will ramp up. Initially, \nshowers will be confined primarily along the Erie lakeshore \novernight tonight until the low slides far enough east by the \npre-dawn hours Saturday morning to flip the flow northwesterly, \na weak impulse moves through, and the coverage of showers pushes\na bit farther south into our area. REFS probability of precip \nmostly confines it north of Pittsburgh and in the PA and WV \nridges with highest chances along I-80 and north. That said, an \nincrease in neighborhood radius to account for spatial \nuncertainty in the hi res guidance does nudge it just a bit \nfarther south as far as Pittsburgh metro. So, have ticked up \nPoPs a bit farther south. This will predominantly be cool, light\nrain showers with two exceptions:\n\n1) The potential for some wet snowflakes to mix in in the pre-dawn \nhours as ground temperatures dip to or just below freezing in the \nridges. The caveat there is that with displacement from the lake \nmoisture fetch, column saturation cuts off well below -10C \nsuggesting that snowflake production is likely going to be hard to \ncome by, so it may be a cool drizzle produced within the supercooled \nliquid region of the cloud bearing layer.\n\n2) Some graupel may mix in especially in the afternoon hours with \nfumes of SBCAPE and near dry adiabatic lapse rates up through the \nsaturated layer.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nThe pressure gradient will tighten up on Sunday on the periphery of \ndeveloping low pressure off to our east. Low level flow in the 925-\n850 mb layer strengthens on the back side of it as well nearing 50 \nknots over the ridges. Dependent on inversion height, exact \ndirection, and potential mixing, Sunday could be gusty across the \narea and especially in the WV ridges. Probability of advisory \ncriteria gusts (>46 mph) reaches up to 70% in Eastern Tucker \nCounty on Sunday. It may be a case that just the far eastern tip\nof the county gets especially gusty as the prevailing flow in \nthe lower levels is westerly and thus favors downsloping to the \neast of our area. Will continue to monitor over the next 24-36 \nhours. In the lowlands, peak gusts being >30 mph are a decent \nbet with probability ranging from 40-80%.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\nThe early weekend trough departs on Sunday and surface high \npressure establishes across the Ohio Valley returning mostly \ndry weather into next week with near normal temperatures. Latest\n12 hour ensemble trends have turned a bit more in favor of the \nsolution dominated by a subset of GEFS and Euro ensemble \nmembers in which the surface high is slower to build, and a \nslightly more amplified shortwave trough slides through early on\nMonday. This would return some scattered showers to the \nnorthern part of our area and NBM PoPs have nudged up in this \ndirection. Regardless, ensembles converge to better agreement \nthat the high does become our dominant influence with dry \nweather on Tuesday. The pattern then becomes very deviant into \nthe second half of the week as another trough tries to dig out \nof the Great Lakes and quickly amplify over the eastern half of \nthe country. How deep it is and when it arrives remain unknown, \nbut one would expect that a more unsettled pattern returns with \nsurface low development somewhere downstream. That may be local \nor well off into the Atlantic at this point. Once we get a \nbetter idea of how deep and how progressive the trough will be, \nwe'll be able to start to pin down the potential location and \ntrack of a surface low.",
